Think about colour psychology:
- Bold/energetic brands → saturated, warm colours
- Luxury brands → muted, deep, dark tones
- Fresh/natural brands → greens, earth tones
- Tech brands → blues, clean neutrals
- Creative/artistic brands → unexpected combinations with personality
- Nigerian/African context → rich, warm palettes feel natural; overly cold/corporate palettes feel disconnected

Return a JSON object with exactly 5 hex colour values:

{
  "primary": "#XXXXXX",
  "secondary": "#XXXXXX",
  "accent": "#XXXXXX",
  "neutral": "#XXXXXX",
  "background": "#XXXXXX"
}

Rules:
- primary: The main brand colour. Must work as a hero colour on web and print.
- secondary: Supports the primary. Different hue or complementary tone.
- accent: For CTAs, highlights, interactive elements. Should pop against the background.
- neutral: For body text and dark backgrounds. Must be dark enough to read comfortably — minimum contrast ratio of 4.5:1 on the background colour.
- background: The page background. Light enough that the neutral text is easily readable on it.

Critical constraints:
- ALL values must be valid 6-digit hex codes with # prefix
- The neutral colour on the background MUST have at least 4.5:1 contrast ratio (WCAG AA)
- The primary colour on the background MUST have at least 4.5:1 contrast ratio
- The accent colour on the background should have at least 3:1 contrast ratio
- No two colours should be visually indistinguishable (delta < 10 in any channel)
- The background should be noticeably lighter than all other colours

Return ONLY the JSON object.